                    MULTITHREADED IP PASCAL - HOW IT WORKS

See the IP Pascal manual for a complete discription of parallel tasking modules.

The module types are:

system
program
module
process
monitor
share

The system module is an internal accouting construction, and does not enter into
tasking. The program and module modules don't have any tasking constructs in 
them.

The share module does not require any special consideration, and does not have
a startup/exit block at all. It is evaluated for code only.

PROCESS MODULES

Process modules are a trick, and they are structured like this:

   newthread(startthread, threadid);
   call modend
   killthread(threadid);
   ret

   startthread:

   <startup code>
   killthread(threadid);
   jmp _

The process module behaves like a normal stacking module, and calls the next
series module, and accepts its return. However, its startup is to start a new
thread that branches off to execute the actual, program specified startup
block. When and if the stack unwinds back to this module, it automatically
kills the thread. The thread will also be killed if it exits, and it is
possible to have the thread terminate and the rest of the program stay
running.

Note that there is no limit on the number of process modules that can be
activated this way.

The threadid for the thread is kept in a global variable that is created
just for this purpose. It is also possible to obtain the thread id by
calling a syslib function.

MONITOR MODULES

Monitors behave like modules as far as the encoder is concerned, but it has the
following special features:

1. It has a global variable which contains its logical lock number.
2. Both the startup and shutdown sections lock and unlock on entry/exit.
3. The logical lock for the monitor is created on startup, before any user code.
4. All procedures and functions in the monitor are locked on entry, and unlocked
on exit using the logical lock number.

For (4), each procedure or function that is globally accessable, meaning that it
could be accessed by an external process, obtains the lock for its monitor on
entry, and gives it up on exit.

The (2) requirement is not technically necessary, since the monitor is 
initialized on the main thread, and the user is not supposed to issue process
modules that up call. It is done for safety, however. It would be bypassed by
a process that starts up and accesses monitor calls before the monitor has
initialized, but this is part of the general upcall issue.

SPECIAL FUNCTIONS AND TYPES

In addition to the special module handling, there are several special system
types, procedures, and functions that will be built into the compiler.

var a: semaphore;

Semaphore variables can appear anywhere a standard variable can. A semaphore
has no value and cannot be set. It can be passed as a parameter, but must be
passed as var. One semaphore cannot be assigned to another. 

Semaphores have the following system procedures that operate on them:

procedure signal(sig: semaphore);

Signals all waiters on a semaphore.

procedure signalone(sig: semaphore);

Signals just one waiter on a signal. This can be more efficient than waking up
all tasks when only one task can use the signal, such as one unit of data only
being available. It is possible for the underlying implementation to actually
signal anywhere from 1 to N, where N is the total number of waiting tasks. In
other words, it must be acceptable to the program to degenerate into the 
signal() case. It is only for efficiency.

procedure wait(sig: semaphore);

Wait for a signal. The calling thread is halted until the signal is flagged by
another thread executing signal() or signalone(). When a wait is executed, the
last lock aquired by the thread is unlocked, then relocked after the wait is
complete. This means that any monitor that calls wait must be prepared for data
changed after both a wait call, or any other call that calls wait indirectly.
Wait does not garantee that the condition that caused the signal will still be
true, since several threads may wake up to the signal and receive whatever
resource the signal was assocated with before the current thread arrives. For
this reason, wait is usually executed in a loop as:

while (condition) do wait(semaphore);

so that the condition that the signal is for is retested each time the wait
exits. Similarly, wait does not garantee "fair queueing", or "first come
first serve". Wait does not garantee efficiency. The wait may be executed by a
polling loop. Finally, wait may unlock the top lock, any number of the last
nested locks, or all the locks held by a thread. This depends on the 
implementation. If no locks are active, then no locks will be freed.

syslib tasking functions

semaphores are represented by an integer variable that contains the logical id
of the semaphore. When a semaphore is created, it has the value 0, which 
indicates to syslib that the semaphore is unconnected to a signal entry. If this
is true, then a new signal entry is created, and the logical number for that is
placed in the semaphore.

Several new functions exist in syslib to support tasking:

procedure ss_newthread(addr: integer; var id: integer);

Creates a new thread with logical id, that begins execution at the given
address. The logical id is 1 to N, where N is commonly a limit of 100. Thread
1 is reserved for the main thread, and is preallocated when the program starts.

procedure ss_killthread(id: integer);

Removes a given logical thread from service, and releases all of its resources.
If the thread holds any locks, they will be released.

procedure ss_signal(var id: integer);

Sends a signal to all of the threads awaiting the given semphore. If the
semaphore is not active, it is created and assigned a logical number.

procedure ss_signalone(var id: integer);

As signal, but may only signal a single thread to execute. This is more 
efficient than waking up all threads.

procedure ss_wait(var id: integer);

Waits for the signal by logical id. The most closely nested lock held by the
thread, if one exists, is released during the wait, and reaquired after.

procedure ss__newlock(var id: integer);

Creates a new lock, and returns the logical id to the caller.

procedure ss__displock(id: integer);

Disposes of an existing lock, by logical id.

procedure ss_lock(var id: integer);

Performs an interprocess lock. If the logical id is 0, then a new lock is 
created, and the logical number placed for the caller.

procedure ss_unlock(var id: integer);

Unlocks an existing lock.

Note that all threads, signals and locks are disposed of when syslib exits.

INTERACTIONS OF THREADS, SIGNALS AND MONITOR LOCKS

The "official" explaination of signals is that waiting for a signal is not
a garantee that another thread would not have grabbed the monitor lock before
the waiting task. Nor are any of the wait or lock functions defined as fair.

The cost of tolerating a possible low quality implementation of tasking is
light in the simple case. A thread executing a wait should execute it in a loop
that checks the case for which it went into the wait, and continues to wait
until it is true.

This solves the simple case of having another "grab" the monitor lock before
the waiter can get it. However, it introduces higher level issues, such as
infinite holdoff of a slow task by a faster one.

To prevent such effects, there are three characteristics of a "good" IP 
tasking implementation that are recommended, if not required. They are:

1. Fair queueing.

2. "heel-to-toe" waiting.

3. Monitor edge signaling.

All three can be implemented strictly at the low level, without high level
compiler or application program support.

FAIR QUEUEING

Fair queuing means that all monitor locks and signals queue on a first come,
first serve basis. This must be regardless of task priority.

HEEL-TO-TOE WAITING

When a wait call is made, logically the monitor lock is dropped, then a loop is
entered to await the signal. This creates two "windows" for other threads to
come in and grab the monitor lock, then presumably take the data or other event
associated with the signal awaited. This would make the waiting task emerge from
the wait without getting it's data, hence the wait loop.

Heel-to-toe waits mean that if a task releases the monitor lock, then waits on
the signal, then again locks, it will be given priority for the lock. There is
effectively a wait queue, and only the task holding the monitor lock can enter
it. When it emerges from the signal, if it does not occupy the next position in
the wait queue, it is sent back to wait on the signal again. In this way, tasks
that examine data state in the monitor, then wait for the data, will be queued
fairly to get the data when it becomes available.

MONITOR EDGE SIGNALING

When a monitor signals a condition, it logically means that the waiters will not
be released to handle it, but rather move from the signal queue to the monitor
lock queue. The reason why is that the monitor lock is, by definition, held by
the signaler, since you have to be in a monitor to signal.

Most monitor routines will signal, then exit the monitor immediately. In monitor
edge signaling, the fact that a signal is at the end of the monitor routine is
detected, and the signal call is a special version that signals and unlocks the
monitor as an atomic step. This both allows the waiting task to proceed directly
through the monitor lock without stopping, and also suppresses other tasks from
grabbing the monitor before the waiter can get to it.

